      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 508,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen** and **oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$H_2O$**—meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, water is much more than a chemical formula. It is the defining substance of our planet and the foundation of all known life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, based on its physical properties, its role on Earth, and its importance to life:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical and Chemical Properties\nWater is unique because of how it behaves under normal Earth conditions:\n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states: **liquid** (oceans, rivers), **solid** (ice, snow), and **gas** (steam, water vapor).\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure, water can dissolve more substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals) than any other liquid. This is why it is essential for transporting nutrients in living things.\n* **Density Anomaly:** Most substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns to ice. This is why ice floats. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat with only small changes in its own temperature. This helps regulate the climate of the Earth and keeps our bodies from overheating.\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\n* **The Blue Planet:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas. \n* **Scarcity of Fresh Water:** Despite how much water is on Earth, about **96.5% of it is salt water** in the oceans. Only about 2.5% is fresh water, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use.\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water is constantly moving in a continuous cycle called the hydrologic cycle. It evaporates from oceans and lakes into the air, condenses into clouds, falls back to Earth as rain or snow, and flows back into bodies of water.\n\n"
